.TH XYMONAPI-MCP 1 "October 30, 2026" "xymon-plugins"
.SH NAME
xymonapi-mcp \- MCP server for the Xymon API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "xymonapi-mcp"
.br
.B "xymonapi-mcp register URL [NAME]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi-mcp gives an AI tool that speaks the Model Context Protocol --
Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Codex and others -- tools to read a Xymon server. The
AI tool starts it and talks to it over stdin and stdout. Each tool is one
request to a running
.I xymonapi(8),
so the AI tool gets what the API answers at that moment: nothing is stored,
and nothing in Xymon is changed. Every tool is marked read-only.

It runs where the AI tool runs, which need not be the Xymon server, and needs
Python 3.9 or later and nothing else: it speaks the protocol with the
standard library, and the searches too are the API's. Without that Python
it says so on stderr and exits with status 77. It writes nothing but protocol
messages on stdout. \fB./install.sh install\fR puts it under a PREFIX of
yours, as the command xymonapi-mcp, with its version in VERSION beside it.
It is written for the xymonapi version its PLUGIN file names; when the
API's /health gives an older one, every answer carries a "warning" field
saying so, and stderr says it once. A newer API is taken as it is. Nothing
is updated on its own, on either side.

.SH REGISTERING
\fBxymonapi-mcp register URL [NAME]\fR connects this machine to the API at
URL with
.I xymonapi-connect(1),
installed beside it -- a password asked once when there is no working token;
nothing, on the server itself, for the API's own socket, unix:PATH -- then
registers the server with Claude Code and with Codex under NAME,
xymon by default: this launcher as the command, with XYMONAPI_URL,
XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE when there is a token, XYMONAPI_CA when a certificate is
used, and XYMONAPI_PYTHON when it is set. A registration of that name is
replaced. For an AI tool that is not installed, it prints the command to run
later. When the connection fails it registers nothing and exits with
xymonapi-connect's status; when an AI tool refuses the registration, it says
which, registers the others all the same, and exits with 1.

.SH TOOLS
.IP "\fBhealth\fR"
Whether xymond answers, and its version.
.IP "\fBstates\fR [entity] [test] [verdict]"
The status board, one state per host and test.
.IP "\fBalarms\fR"
Every state whose verdict is not ok.
.IP "\fBentities\fR"
Each host on the board, once.
.IP "\fBstatus\fR entity test"
The current status of one host's test, with its text.
.IP "\fBseries\fR entity test [metric] [start] [end]"
The values of one dataset of a host's RRD file over a time range.
.IP "\fBsearch_docs\fR question [k]"
The passages of Xymon's manual pages nearest the question, by meaning.
.IP "\fBsearch_history\fR question [k]"
The past colour changes nearest the question, with the status text saved at
each.
.LP
The first six answer what the xymonapi(8) operation of the same name
answers; the two searches its /search, from the index the API serves.
That page says what each field means. An API
that cannot be reached, or that refuses a request, is a tool error whose
text says which, and what to check first; so is a search on a server with
no index.

.SH SECURITY
search_history returns status texts that the monitored hosts wrote, each
marked as data, not instructions; a host that is broken into can still write
there words meant to steer the AI tool's model. Every tool here only reads,
but an AI tool that also has tools that change things should be watched
when it acts on a history it found.

The user and password are sent in the clear over http://: across a network,
give an https:// XYMONAPI_URL -- the API's own, when it has xymond's
certificate, or the web server's in front of it, as xymonapi(8) says --
and XYMONAPI_CA when the certificate is not one this machine trusts.
Redirects are not followed, so the credentials go to XYMONAPI_URL and
nowhere else. On the server itself, the socket sends no credential and
crosses no network.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_URL\fR"
Where xymonapi(8) is: an http:// or https:// URL, the host and, when it is
not the scheme's own, the port -- 1986 for the API's own HTTPS, the web
server's for one in front of it -- without /xymon/api/v1, which is added; or
unix:PATH, the API's own
socket on the server, where no token is needed (xymonapi(8),
XYMONAPI_SOCKET). The default is that socket when XYMONAPI_SOCKET, or
XYMONVAR, says where it is and it is there, and http://127.0.0.1:1986
otherwise.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN\fR"
A client token, from the first line of the file XYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE names,
or XYMONAPI_TOKEN itself: this machine's own credential (xymonapi(8),
TOKENS), and the only one sent; no password is kept on this machine.
xymonapi-connect(1) keeps one, and register names its file here.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_CA\fR"
For an https:// XYMONAPI_URL whose certificate this machine does not trust
already: the server's certificate, or the CA that signed it, PEM. Xymond's
own certificate, when the API uses it (xymon-tls(7)), is such a one.
Verification is never turned off: the token rides on this connection.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
The Python to run with. Unset, the python3 of the virtualenv beside the
plugins, $PREFIX/libexec/xymon-plugins/venv, which \fB./install.sh venv\fR
makes, when there is one; else the first of python3, python3.14 down to
python3.9, found on PATH. Nothing is
asked: this program needs no module beyond the standard library.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonapi(8), xymonapi-connect(1), xymonapi-rag(1)
